<h3>All three original equivalent-or-not exercises</h3>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Source pair</th><th>Equivalent?</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>1/2 vs 1/3</th><td>No</td><td>3/6 is greater than2/6</td></tr>
<tr><th>4/6 vs5/6</th><td>No</td><td>4/6 is smaller than5/6 of SAME unit</td></tr>
<tr><th>3/4 vs6/8</th><td>Yes</td><td>3×8=6×4=24</td></tr>
</tbody></table><h3>Han and Lin: do not compare unequal source drawing scales</h3>
<p>Han uses two source number lines whose 0–1 whole units are identical in width: 4/6 lies LEFT of 5/6. Lin's number lines BOTH show six equal spaces but their complete 0–1 units are unequal in drawn width, causing a mistaken physical comparison. The source correct conclusion is <strong>4/6 &lt; 5/6</strong> when both refer to the same whole.</p>
<p>Teacher verifies pupils explain why Lin's scales are invalid and support comparisons with diagrams. No publisher SVG bytes or gated Cool-down reproduced. <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-5/lesson-14/lesson.html">Original 2021 teacher page</a>.</p></section>